d from swift the international  payment system  and russia's central bank cannot access  around half of its foreign reserves  several multinationals have also decided  to quit the market  as part of a boycott making it even less  attractive to do business in russia  now let's bring in timothy ash he's a  senior sovereign strategist for emerging  markets at blue bay asset management tim  thanks so much for joining us so we did  see russian  the russian benchmark index rise today  what do you make of that  well it's pretty artificial uh firstly  short selling of stock is not allowed  that's a very limited  section of stocks  for uh for sale or you can trade  foreigners allowed to trade them and the  russian sovereign wealth fund has uh has  basically put it's put aside 10 billion  dollars to buy stocks and this has  really been artificially maintained  because russia wants to portray an image  that there's stability and it's it's uh  it's regained  what regains stability on its financial  markets  yeah i guess you mentioned 10 billion  dollars that the russian sovereign  wealth fund has used to help prop up the  market russia is obviously pretty cash  strapped right now what is what is the  purpose of of using that precious  foreign exchange that precious cash to  prop up stocks  well they they want to send a general  message to the population of of  stability and confidence remember uh the  the the the invasion the sanctions came  as a massive shock to russia's  population they believed the lies of the  kremlin that they weren't going to  invade so the biggest holders of russian  financial assets are russians themselves  foreign has sold out so russians were  under massive amount of pain because of  because of sanctions in the collapse of  russian financial markets and there was  concern about a run on banks and a  broader collapse of macro financial  stability so so it's all about sending a  message that things are okay everyone  needs to calm down the russian  authorities have got everything in  control but it's as i said it's  artificial it's artificially maintained  the longer term story is dreadful  sanctions gonna remain in place for a  very long time the outlook for the  russian economy is terrible there's no  real reason to invest in russian assets  at this point in time  there also um  is continuing discussions here in the eu  really around the world  about what further sanctions should be  put into place what do you see coming  next in terms of sanctions  well i think energy should be sanctioned  and germany's central there because for  years it's been appeasing putin and it's  depend it's over dependency on any on  russian energy is its own fault right  sure  sanctioning russian energy would have a  big hit to the german economy growth  would slow but germany has the strongest  fiscal in the eu it can offset the the  recessionary impacts of of banning  russian energy by pump priming growth  with fiscal channel you know i think  this is time for germany to stand up  actually it's uh you know energy  sanctions are the one one that's the key  angle that the west can hurt putin and  maybe stop in with this invasion on the  of ukraine  there has obviously been a lot of  resistance to that here in germany which  is one of the biggest eu importers of  natural gas  do you get a sense that that that  resistance is shifting at this point  well if you think about sanctions there  was a lot of resistance to swift  sanctions from germany amongst other  countries  uh resistance to cbr sanctions uh  remember a lot of these companies i mean  self-sanctioning has been the new thing  that companies have been embarrassed uh  by the fact that they're still doing  business with russia and and you know  putin the war criminal and the stuff  that he's been doing in ukraine um i  think the embarrassment is a massive  factor of  working towards momentum in terms of  sanctions and i think in in the end the  german government itself could be  